[0] M2-c001 L01 grade=None basis=None
   F: What is a sequence?
   B: A list (a_1, a_2, a_3, and so on) with one real number a_n for each positive integer n. It is written (a_n) and its terms are the numbers a_n (Section 1).
[1] M2-c002 L01 grade=None basis=None
   F: What is the limit of a sequence (Definition 2.1)?
   B: L is the limit of (a_n), written a_n -> L, when for every epsilon > 0 there is a positive integer N with |a_n - L| < epsilon for every integer n >= N. A sequence with a limit is convergent; a sequence with no limit is divergent.
[2] M2-c003 L01 grade=None basis=None
   F: In Definition 2.1, which quantity is chosen first?
   B: The tolerance epsilon, which may be any positive number. The index N is then chosen to depend on epsilon, and the inequality must hold from N onward, not only at N (Definition 2.1).
[3] M2-c004 L01 grade=T basis=None
   F: Can a sequence have two different limits?
   B: No. Proposition 2.2: if (a_n) is a sequence of real numbers and both L and M satisfy Definition 2.1 for it, then L = M. The proof takes thresholds for epsilon/2 and the triangle inequality at n = max(N_1, N_2) to get |L - M| < epsilon for every epsilon > 0.
[4] M2-c005 L01 grade=T basis=None
   F: For a_n = (2n+1)/(n+1), what threshold works for a tolerance epsilon?
   B: Any positive integer N > 1/epsilon. Then a_n - 2 = -1/(n+1), so |a_n - 2| = 1/(n+1) < epsilon for every n >= N, and a_n -> 2 (Example 2.3).
[5] M2-c006 L01 grade=C basis='Lab 1, a finite search over 1 to 5000; the identity |a_n - 2| = 1/(n+1) in Example 2.3'
   F: In Lab 1, what is the least N with |a_n - 2| < 0.001 for every n from N to 5000?
   B: N = 1000. The error at n = 999 is 1.000000e-03, so the strict inequality fails there, and the error at n = 1000 is 9.990010e-04.
[6] M2-c007 L01 grade=None basis=None
   F: Does a finite search over terms prove a limit?
   B: No. Lab 1 checks finitely many n for one tolerance. The limit claim for every tolerance and every n is proved by Example 2.3, and no search over finitely many n replaces that proof (Section 5).
[7] M2-c008 L01 grade=T basis=None
   F: If two sequences agree from index M onward, do they have the same limit?
   B: Yes, if one has a limit. Proposition 3.1: if a_n -> L and c_n = a_n for every n >= M, then c_n -> L. The first M - 1 terms of (c_n) are unrestricted.
[8] M2-c009 L01 grade=T basis=None
   F: Does (-1)^n converge?
   B: No. It is bounded by 1. If (-1)^n tends to a limit L, take epsilon = 1 and an even n and an odd m beyond the threshold: then |1 - L| < 1 and |-1 - L| < 1, so 2 = |1 - (-1)| < 2, which is false (Example 4.1, proof by contradiction).
[9] M2-c010 L01 grade=None basis=None
   F: Must a sequence with limit L reach L at some finite step?
   B: No. Definition 2.1 asks only that the terms eventually lie within epsilon of L for each epsilon. The sequence 1/n has limit 0, and no term equals 0 (Misconception 1).
[10] M2-c011 L01 grade=T basis=None
   F: Do the first terms of a convergent sequence help determine its limit?
   B: No. For a convergent sequence, Proposition 3.1 shows that changing finitely many terms leaves the limit unchanged. Replacing the first term of (2n+1)/(n+1) by 10^6 leaves the limit 2 (Misconception 2).
[11] M2-c012 L01 grade=T basis=None
   F: Is a bounded sequence convergent?
   B: No. The sequence (-1)^n satisfies |a_n| <= 1 for every n and does not converge. Boundedness is necessary for a limit, not sufficient (Example 4.1, Misconception 3).
[12] M2-c013 L01 grade=T basis=None
   F: Are the terms of (2n+1)/(n+1) ever equal to their limit 2?
   B: No. Setting (2n+1)/(n+1) = 2 gives 2n + 1 = 2n + 2, which has no solution (Section 5).
[13] M2-c014 L02 grade=None basis=None
   F: Under what hypotheses does Proposition 1.1 apply?
   B: Both (a_n) and (b_n) are sequences of real numbers with a_n -> a and b_n -> b, where a and b are real numbers, each limit existing in the sense of Definition 2.1 of Lesson 1.
[14] M2-c015 L02 grade=T basis=None
   F: Proposition 1.1 (i): what is the sum law?
   B: If a_n -> a and b_n -> b, then a_n + b_n -> a + b. Proof: choose N_1 and N_2 for epsilon/2 and apply the triangle inequality at n >= max(N_1, N_2).
[15] M2-c016 L02 grade=T basis=None
   F: Proposition 1.1 (ii): what is the product law?
   B: If a_n -> a and b_n -> b, then a_n b_n -> ab. The proof writes a_n b_n - ab = a_n(b_n - b) + b(a_n - a) and bounds |a_n| by |a| + 1 for large n.
[16] M2-c017 L02 grade=T basis=None
   F: Proposition 1.1 (iii): what does the quotient law require?
   B: b != 0 and b_n != 0 for every n. Then a_n / b_n -> a / b. The hypothesis b != 0 keeps |b_n| bounded away from zero for large n, and b_n != 0 makes each quotient defined.
[17] M2-c018 L02 grade=T basis=None
   F: Is the limit of a quotient the quotient of the limits, whatever the denominator does?
   B: No. Remark 3.1 takes a_n = 1 and b_n = 1/n. The denominator converges to 0, and the quotient a_n / b_n = n diverges. Proposition 1.1 (iii) excludes this case by its hypothesis b != 0 (Misconception 1).
[18] M2-c019 L02 grade=T basis=None
   F: Does a sum of two divergent sequences always diverge?
   B: No. With a_n = (-1)^n and b_n = -(-1)^n, each term diverges, but a_n + b_n = 0 for every n, so the sum converges to 0 (Misconception 2). Proposition 1.1 gives only one direction.
[19] M2-c020 L02 grade=None basis=None
   F: What is continuity at a point a of D in the sequential sense?
   B: f is continuous at a when f(x_n) -> f(a) for every sequence (x_n) in D with x_n -> a (Section 2).
[20] M2-c021 L02 grade=T basis=None
   F: Is the identity f(x) = x continuous at every point?
   B: Yes. If x_n -> a, then f(x_n) = x_n -> a = f(a) by the definition of the limit (Example 2.1).
[21] M2-c022 L02 grade=T basis=None
   F: Corollary 2.2: are sums and products of continuous functions continuous?
   B: Yes. If f and g are both continuous at a point a of D, then f + g and fg are continuous at a. The hypothesis is continuity in the sequential sense of Section 2. The proof applies Proposition 1.1 (i) and (ii) along each sequence.
[22] M2-c023 L02 grade=T basis=None
   F: Is x squared continuous at every point?
   B: Yes. The identity is continuous at each point (Example 2.1), and Corollary 2.2 applied to the identity with itself gives continuity of x times x (Example 2.3).
[23] M2-c024 L02 grade=T basis=None
   F: Does a limit at a point make a function continuous there?
   B: No. Take f(x) = x + 1 for x != 1 and f(1) = 0. With x_n = 1 + 1/n, f(x_n) -> 2, but f(1) = 0, so f is not continuous at 1 (Misconception 3).
