[26] M2-c027 L03 grade=None basis=None
   F: What does lim_{x -> a} f(x) = L mean (Definition 1.1)?
   B: For every epsilon > 0 there is delta > 0 with |f(x) - L| < epsilon for every x in I with 0 < |x - a| < delta. The value f(a), if it exists, plays no role in this limit.
[27] M2-c028 L03 grade=T basis=None
   F: Is lim_{x -> 2} (3x + 1) equal to 7?
   B: Yes. Example 1.2 takes delta = epsilon/3; if 0 < |x - 2| < delta then |f(x) - 7| = 3|x - 2| < 3 delta = epsilon.
[28] M2-c029 L03 grade=T basis=None
   F: Proposition 1.3: what does a function limit give along sequences?
   B: Hypotheses: lim_{x -> a} f(x) = L in the sense of Definition 1.1, and (x_n) is a sequence in I with each x_n not equal to a and x_n -> a. Then f(x_n) -> L. The proof uses delta from Definition 1.1 and the fact that x_n differs from a.
[29] M2-c030 L03 grade=T basis=None
   F: Corollary 1.4: when is f continuous at a?
   B: When f is defined on I, a is in I, and lim_{x -> a} f(x) = f(a) in the sense of Definition 1.1. Then f is continuous at a in the sequential sense of Lesson 2, Section 2. The proof handles the case x_n = a separately.
[30] M2-c031 L03 grade=None basis=None
   F: Definition 2.1: what are the one-sided limits?
   B: lim_{x -> a+} f(x) = L (right-hand) means that for every epsilon > 0 there is delta > 0 with |f(x) - L| < epsilon for every x in I with a < x < a + delta. The left-hand limit lim_{x -> a-} f(x) = L uses a - delta < x < a.
[31] M2-c032 L03 grade=T basis=None
   F: Proposition 2.2: when does the two-sided limit exist?
   B: Hypotheses: f is defined on an open interval I containing a, except possibly at a, I contains a in its interior, and L is real. Then lim_{x -> a} f(x) = L if and only if both one-sided limits at a equal L.
[32] M2-c033 L03 grade=T basis=None
   F: Does the piecewise function f(x) = x^2 for x < 1 and f(x) = 2x for x >= 1 have a two-sided limit at 1?
   B: No. Its left-hand limit at 1 is 1 and its right-hand limit at 1 is 2. The two differ, so by Proposition 2.2 the two-sided limit does not exist (Example 2.3).
[33] M2-c034 L03 grade=T basis=None
   F: Does g(x) = (x^2 - 1)/(x - 1) have a limit at 1 when g(1) is undefined?
   B: Yes, lim_{x -> 1} g(x) = 2. For x not equal to 1, g(x) = x + 1, so |g(x) - 2| = |x - 1| and delta = epsilon works (Example 2.4).
