[50] M2-c051 L05 grade=T basis=None
   F: Lemma 1.1: what limit laws hold as h tends to 0?
   B: Hypotheses: F and G are defined for 0 < |h| < r, F(h) -> L and G(h) -> M as h -> 0, with L and M real. Then F + G -> L + M, FG -> LM, and, if M is not 0, F/G -> L/M. The proof repeats Lesson 2, Proposition 1.1 with h in place of n.
[51] M2-c052 L05 grade=T basis=None
   F: Product rule (Proposition 1.2): what is (fg)'(a)?
   B: Hypotheses: f and g are differentiable at a. Then fg is differentiable at a and (fg)'(a) = f'(a) g(a) + f(a) g'(a). The proof adds and subtracts f(a+h) g(a) inside the difference quotient.
[52] M2-c053 L05 grade=T basis=None
   F: Quotient rule (Proposition 1.3): what is (f/g)'(a)?
   B: Hypotheses: f and g are differentiable at a, and g(a) is not 0. Then f/g is defined near a, differentiable at a, and (f/g)'(a) = (f'(a) g(a) - f(a) g'(a)) / g(a)^2.
[53] M2-c054 L05 grade=T basis=None
   F: Chain rule (Proposition 2.1): what is (g o f)'(a)?
   B: Hypotheses: f is differentiable at a in I, g is differentiable at b = f(a) in J, and f(I) is contained in J. Then (g o f)'(a) = g'(f(a)) f'(a). The outer derivative is evaluated at f(a), not at a.
